  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a design map for cage receptors that bind saccharides, organized by both cage type and the medium in which they work best. Across the top are covalent molecular cage receptors, shown in three broad classes: neutral cages associated with organic media, anionic cages spanning toward aqueous use, and cationic cages associated with aqueous media. The charged decorations around the cage drawings visually suggest how adding negative or positive groups tunes solubility and binding behavior.\n\nThe lower half shows coordination cage receptors, where assembly is driven by different components rather than a fully covalent framework. These include neutral molecule-driven assemblies, non-metallic anion-driven assemblies, and metal cation-driven assemblies. Their placement across the figure implies a progression from systems suited to organic environments toward ones compatible with water.\n\nOverall, the main message is that saccharide-binding cage receptors can be built through either covalent or coordination strategies, and that neutral, anionic, or cationic character strongly influences the kind of medium they operate in. The diagram highlights a conceptual shift from neutral organic cages in organic solvents to increasingly charged architectures, especially cationic ones, for aqueous saccharide recognition.",
